@meta
id: 02-02
title: Two keys to write and quit
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: write, quit
par: 14
minutes: 3
vscode: Ctrl+S then Ctrl+W
open: refund.service.ts

@brief
`:wq` is four keystrokes for something you do hundreds of times a day.
Normal mode has a two-key version: `ZZ`, capital Z twice. Write the file
if it changed, then quit.

The refund call is missing its semicolon. Add it, and leave with `ZZ`.

Nothing else in this file needs touching. Adding a character to the end of
a line and getting out should cost you fewer than a dozen keystrokes.

@keys
| `ZZ` | write if modified, then quit |
| `:x` | the same thing, spelled as an ex command |
| `:wq` | write always, then quit |

@hints
The line you want is the `return` inside `refund`. It is five rows down.
--
`$` then `a` puts you in insert mode past the last character of the line.
--
`Esc` first, then two capital Zs. No colon, no Enter — `ZZ` is a normal
mode command, not a command line one.

@solution
    jjjjj $ a ; <Esc> ZZ

Eleven keystrokes. `:wq` would have made it thirteen.

`ZZ` and `:x` are the same command: write only if the buffer was modified,
then quit. `:wq` differs in one small way — it writes unconditionally, so
an unchanged file gets a new modification time. That matters more than it
sounds like it should. `make`, `tsc --watch`, nodemon and every file
watcher key off modification times, so a stray `:wq` on a file you only
read can kick off a rebuild. Use `ZZ` by default.

The `Z` family is worth knowing as a pair: `ZZ` saves and goes, `ZQ` is
the opposite — quit and throw the changes away. Reach for `ZQ` only when
you are certain, because there is no confirmation.

@start refund.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  public async refund(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    return this.paymentGateway.post(`/refunds`, args)
  }
}

@expected refund.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  public async refund(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    return this.paymentGateway.post(`/refunds`, args);
  }
}
